ter you're  actually in a 3d environment like fully  3d not just for the rendering but also  for the gameplay but back then what was  happening is that we forgot that we uh  our plans for aiming system  was based on uh on a 2d system  and even some some twin stick shooters  that are actually rendered in 3d they  are still operate on one plan that's the  thing that  did not click with us with any of us  twin stick shooters operate on the core  principle that one analog stick moves  your character while the other aims  where you shoot  and that's it and that control scheme  has remained unchanged since the genre's  popularization with robotron back in  1982  there's a great game by the way now it  may have evolved from flat pixels to  fully 3d worlds but how they operate  function and move has remained the same  you point  and you shoot  weird west on the other hand  well they wanted to do things a little  different weirder even  as we implemented our our aiming system  it was quickly  uh  hell we quickly realized that we were  stuck uh because there was no game that  had pulled that off there was no game  where there were elevations and at the  same time you could aim anywhere the  same way you can in a twisted shooter  the aiming feature we thought would  probably plan like maybe four weeks on  it but unfortunately it probably took  more like four to six months  even more the problem arose one year  into the game's development putting raph  and his team  a pretty tough spot  it was out of the question to do to not  let the player aim at anything and at  the same time at this point was out of  the question to go back in 2d and that's  there is moment where you really think  are we screwed did we corner ourselves  in a way where  that's it we'll  never be able to make the game that we  were set to to do  the team tried a lot of different  solutions they experimented with a cone  system where instead of aiming a thin  line of sight the player instead  directed a cone r